Paper-as-protocol era (1989–1998)
LeCun et al., "Gradient-Based Learning Applied to Document Recognition" (Proc. IEEE, 1998) — LeNet-5 and MNIST in one document. Every layer, every parameter, in tables in the paper. This is the last era where the fax was the repo. It's also, as noted, the honest ancestor of exactly the architecture you reconstructed. [High confidence.] With it, the 1989 zip-code paper (Neural Computation) — complete at 1989 scale.

Protocol-as-config era (2012–now)
torchvision reference training scripts (pytorch/vision, references/classification): the de facto canonical ResNet training protocol — augmentation, schedule, epochs, all specified in runnable code. This is the document you run when you want "actual ResNet." [High confidence.]
Wightman et al., "ResNet Strikes Back: An Improved Training Procedure in timm" (arXiv 2110.00476, ~2021): took canonical ResNet-50 and showed the training recipe alone moves it from ~76% to ~80%+ — with the full recipe and per-run logs in the timm results tables. This is the architecture-is-not-the-method paper, and it's the direct theoretical support for your fitness-plateau finding. [High confidence on existence; verify authors.] You want a control for "the recipe is the method" — this is it.
He et al., "Bag of Tricks for Image Classification Training" (CVPR 2019): the closest thing to a modern methods-section-as-paper — each trick isolated, ablated, exact settings. [High confidence.]
Russakovsky et al., "ImageNet LSVRC" (IJCV, 2015): the evaluation-protocol authority — exact splits, exact rules — the same role the ISIC challenge papers play in your corpus's universe. [High confidence.]
Historical origin of the tradition: the Caffe Model Zoo (~2014), the first "weights + config as publishable method" registry, and its modern descendants — the MMDetection/OpenMMLab config files, which are literally exhaustive protocol documents checked into version control. [Medium-high; verify.]
